  "text": "A brief, self‑filled instrument for assessing cognitive decline was created. It takes about twenty minutes to complete and only a couple of minutes to score, and it can be used with individuals or groups. The test combines a word‑knowledge section with a problem‑solving section that requires abstract reasoning. Cognitive loss is judged by comparing the abstract‑reasoning score to the word‑knowledge level, expressed as a special ratio that shows how a person’s reasoning ability stacks up against the average person with the same vocabulary. The tool was calibrated using data from more than a thousand people whose standard intelligence scores were known, allowing the conversion of raw scores into age‑equivalent levels for each part and for the combined result. When administered to several hundred patients from two types of treatment centers, the scores were markedly lower than those of the normal group, especially in the more severe diagnostic categories. The findings support the instrument’s usefulness for detecting intellectual impairment, particularly in early‑stage cases with average or above‑average baseline ability."
}
